after Steve Jobs and Tim Cook the most  recognizable face at Apple probably this  one Jonathan Ive Apple's chief designer  and the man behind products like the  iMac the iPod the iPhone the iPad and  recently iOS 7 Apple's latest mobile  operating system I doesn't strut around  on stage like a lot of other Apple  executives he's usually seen only in  videos and he very rarely gives  interviews except recently he gave one  to me and being that he is probably the  greatest industrial designer of his  generation I wanted to share some of his  comments with you the discoveries you  make when you are lucky enough to sit  next to somebody who represents a  completely different expertise those  discoveries can be really profound  apples not a terribly siloed  organization sure it has different  buildings but people tend to work in ad  hoc groups  I for example sits only one minute away  from Craig federighi Apple's chief of  software and often times the  distinctions between the two teams are  known only by HR I think we feel our job  is to try to solve tough difficult  problems but we don't make the  complexity of the problem apparent in  its resolution Ives philosophy is not  show your work the things his team works  on can be wickedly complex take these  animations in iOS 7 for example but to  the end-user they have to be dead simple  we feel that we've done our job when you  finally get to that point and you think  well there couldn't be a rational  alternative it appears inevitable it  almost appears like it wasn't designed  I've hear echoes the principles of  fellow industrial designer dieter roms  who designed products for Braun in the  60s 70s and 80s good design said roms is  as little design as possible  you
 

